"headerTitle" = "Create a Project Folder";
"helpText" = "## How to use\n1. In Finder or Path Finder, bring the window of the place for the new folder to the front.\n2. Open the folder form from the folder icon in the menu bar, by launching the app from the Dock or Spotlight, or with your hotkey.\n3. Enter the client, project, date and note (all optional). The folder name is shown below.\n4. Click Create Folder (Return). Cancel (Esc) closes the window.\n\n## Folder name\n- Client-Region-YYYYMMDD-Note, for example ricoh-osaka-20261014-visit.\n- Client, project and note are optional; when left out, so is their “-”.\n- With Today on, today's date is used. With it off, the Year, Month and Day fields are used.\n- The year starts as this year through October and next year from November (as in the Keyboard Maestro macro).\n- Month and day are padded to two digits (3 → 03). Without a day the date is YYYYMM; without a month, YYYY.\n- “/”, “:”, line breaks and a leading “.” are not allowed.\n- Client, region, location and Today are remembered; month, day and note start empty.\n\n## Bulk mode\n- Switch to Bulk at the top for a large text area. Each typed or pasted line becomes one folder.\n- Blank lines are ignored; leading “- ”, “* ”, “・”, “•”, “1.” and “1)” are removed; repeated lines are made once.\n- “/” makes folders inside (for example ricoh-osaka-20261014/assets).\n- A leading “/” puts the line inside the one above, “//” one level deeper; tabs or spaces work the same (as in project structures).\n- Names with an extension (memo.txt, site/index.html) are made as files, copied from a template when there is one (“index.html < html5.html” picks one). {{project}} is the line's top folder, or the destination's name. Add “/” to a dotted folder name (example.com/).\n- A leading “-” works like “/”. A heading followed by lines starting with “+” gives names of the heading and the item joined by a space; the heading itself is not made. A blank line or the next heading ends the group.\n- “A...C”, “1...10” and “01...12” (or “…”) expand as ranges; zero padding is kept. Example: Project / +A...C → Project A, Project B, Project C. “*” counts: takano-4*3 → takano-4, -5, -6; takano-*3 → takano-1 to -3.\n- The list below shows which lines are new, already exist or are unusable before you create them. Existing folders are skipped (never overwritten).\n- Return makes a line break in the text area, so create with ⌘Return or Create All.\n- The location is the same as for a single folder. Afterwards the location opens with the first new folder selected. No AT-Recent link or Path Finder sidebar item is made.\n- When everything was created, the text is cleared and the window closes as set; otherwise the text stays with the reasons.\n\n## Projects\n- Choose Project at the top to create a folder structure saved in Settings.\n- Pick a project and enter a folder name; the structure is made inside it (empty: straight into the location). Existing folders are skipped.\n- Settings > Projects adds (+), deletes (−), duplicates and edits projects. One item per line; levels by indentation (tabs, or spaces — the shallowest space indent is one level) or leading “/”. Names with an extension (style.css, index.html) are made as empty files; add “/” to a folder name with a dot (v1.0/). Existing files are never overwritten.\n\n## File templates\n- Files in projects are copied from templates in ~/Library/Application Support/FolderMaker/Templates (like Post Haste's bundled documents). Settings > Projects > Open Templates Folder opens it.\n- Order: the name after “<” in the structure line (index.html < html5.html), the same file name, then template.<ext>. Otherwise the file is empty.\n- {{project}}, {{file}} and {{date}} in text templates are filled in. Bundled as real, openable blank documents (not 0 KB): docx, xlsx, pptx, pdf and ai (A4; ai is PDF-compatible), png, jpg and psd (1920×1080 white), svg, rtf, json, xml, csv, txt, and html, css, scss, js, md starters. Put blank documents for other formats there yourself (template.indd).\n- Updates never overwrite the folder; Restore Bundled Templates brings back deleted ones.\n\n## Naming\n- Settings > Naming changes the single-folder pattern ({client}-{project}-{date}-{memo}), the date format and the field names. An empty item is left out with its separator.\n- Write names in NFC (on by default) avoids broken names in Dropbox and Windows. Do not make names Windows cannot use (on by default).\n- On a case-sensitive volume “ABC” and “abc” are different folders.\n\n## One Folder with a project\n- Also make a project structure makes the chosen project inside the new folder. {{project}} and {{date}} are filled in names too ({{project}}.psd).\n\n## Undo\n- Undo after creating, File > Undo Last Creation (⌥⌘Z) or the menu bar menu moves what was just made to the Trash. Changed items stay; the AT-Recent link is removed; Path Finder's sidebar item stays. Only the last creation, until you quit.\n\n## Sharing\n- Choose a shared folder (Dropbox …) in Settings > Projects to keep projects, the naming rule and templates there for several Macs. Changes from other Macs are read when you return to the app; the last save wins.\n\n## Location\n- Choose one of three locations with the radio buttons.\n  - Chosen folder: the folder picked with Choose… (the same value as Settings > Location).\n  - Front folder: the folder of the Finder or Path Finder window that is higher on screen. It is read when the form opens and again just before creating. File > Re-read Front Folder (⌘R) reads it again.\n  - Recent folder: pick from Finder's and Path Finder's history (up to 15, newest first). The last choice is remembered.\n- If an item with the same name exists, nothing is created (nothing is overwritten).\n\n## After creating\nChoose in Settings > After Creating.\n- Open the new folder (on by default) with Automatic (Path Finder when it is running, else Finder), Path Finder or Finder. It can also be selected in its parent folder instead.\n- Close the window after creating (on by default). If a step did not finish, the window stays open with the reason.\n- Add to Path Finder's sidebar (off by default): selects the folder in Path Finder and chooses File > Add to Sidebar.\n- Symbolic link (One Folder mode only): puts a link with the same name in a folder such as AT-Recent. An old link is replaced; a real file or folder is left alone.\n\n## Hotkey\n- Set a key in Settings > Startup & Background to show the form (press again to close). Default: ⇧⌥⌘N. It works only while Finder or Path Finder (or ProjectFolder) is in front; in other apps the key goes to that app.\n- Press a key with ⌘, ⌥ or ⌃ to record it. Esc cancels; Clear Key removes it.\n- If another app uses the key, registering may fail and the reason is shown.\n- This app replaces the Keyboard Maestro macro “Folder Maker（案件）”. If you use the same key, disable the macro.\n\n## Settings\n- Startup & Background: launch at login (off by default), hide main window at startup, keep running, menu bar icon, the hotkey, Accessibility and Automation, Restart and Quit.\n- Location: the default location.\n- After Creating: opening, closing, Path Finder's sidebar, symbolic link.\n- About: version and links.\n- ⌘, opens Settings and ⌘W closes it.\n\n## Permissions\n- Automation: used to ask Finder and Path Finder for the front folder and to select the folder in Path Finder. Choose OK when asked.\n- Accessibility: needed only for Add to Path Finder's sidebar. Turn on ProjectFolder in System Settings > Privacy & Security > Accessibility.\n\n## Running in the background and quitting\n- The folder icon in the menu bar offers the main window, Settings, Help and Quit. Hide it with Menu Bar Settings… in the app menu.\n- Closing the window keeps the app running. Reopening it from the Dock or Finder shows the main window.\n- Quit with Quit ProjectFolder (app menu, menu bar or Settings) or ⌘Q while the app is in front.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- “No Finder or Path Finder window is open”: open a folder window and click Re-read. Recents and search windows have no folder.\n- “Not allowed”: turn on Finder and Path Finder for ProjectFolder in System Settings > Privacy & Security > Automation.\n- Not added to the sidebar: allow Accessibility and check that Path Finder's File menu has Add to Sidebar.\n- Still shown as not allowed: relaunch the app; if that does not help, remove ProjectFolder from the list in System Settings and add it again.\n\n## Undo\nThe new folder is empty; move it to the Trash if you do not need it. Remove the symbolic link and the sidebar item the same way.\n\n## Privacy\nThere is no network communication (update checks stay off until a feed is set up). Only the client and region are saved, in this Mac's preferences.\n\n## Removing\nDelete ProjectFolder from Applications. To remove its settings, delete ~/Library/Preferences/jp.dtp-transit.foldermaker.plist and remove it from Login Items, Accessibility and Automation.";
